WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.315
Lesson 12.5.

00:00:02.315 --> 00:00:06.391
Being the lender: supplying, curating and pricing credit risk.

00:00:06.391 --> 00:00:08.486
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:08.486 --> 00:00:10.467
able to act as the lending side of

00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:13.071
the bank: choose markets, price the risk, and

00:00:13.071 --> 00:00:14.656
know when to pull liquidity.

00:00:15.956 --> 00:00:17.246
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.246 --> 00:00:18.931
First, the key ideas.

00:00:18.931 --> 00:00:21.646
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:23.171
Then, your checklist.

00:00:23.171 --> 00:00:25.333
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:28.897
When you supply to a money market, you're

00:00:28.897 --> 00:00:32.108
the bank's depositor, and effectively its credit desk.

00:00:32.108 --> 00:00:35.935
Your return: supply APY ≈ borrow APY

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:40.063
× utilisation × (1 − reserve factor).

00:00:40.063 --> 00:00:45.175
Modern designs split lending into isolated markets (one collateral,

00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:49.158
one loan asset, one oracle, one liquidation LTV).

00:00:49.158 --> 00:00:52.152
Curated vaults spread deposits across several of

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:55.018
these; the curator decides the allocation.

00:00:55.018 --> 00:00:58.546
What a lender must check: First, Collateral quality

00:00:58.546 --> 00:01:01.599
and liquidation LTV: the higher the LTV, the

00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:04.273
thinner the buffer before bad debt.

00:01:04.273 --> 00:01:06.827
Next, Oracle: what prices the collateral,

00:01:06.827 --> 00:01:08.828
and can it be manipulated?

00:01:08.828 --> 00:01:10.723
(Module 5.3).

00:01:10.723 --> 00:01:13.633
Then, Utilisation: near 100% means you

00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:16.023
may not be able to withdraw.

00:01:16.023 --> 00:01:20.998
Also, Curator: track record, how allocations are changed, timelocks.

00:01:20.998 --> 00:01:24.063
And finally, Risk-adjusted return, not headline.

00:01:25.363 --> 00:01:27.878
Let's work an example, step

00:01:27.878 --> 00:01:31.776
by step. Vault A: 7% headline, exposure to newer

00:01:31.776 --> 00:01:35.595
collateral; you assume a 2%/yr chance of a loss

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:39.953
event losing half the deposit → 6.0% risk-adjusted.

00:01:39.953 --> 00:01:43.960
Vault B: 4.5% headline, blue-chip collateral; 0.5%/yr,

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.019
total loss assumed → 4.0%. the calculator

00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:49.723
A pays more after the haircut, if

00:01:49.723 --> 00:01:52.267
your loss estimates are honest.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.713
Split between them, and cap

00:01:54.713 --> 00:01:56.893
A lower because its tail

00:01:56.893 --> 00:01:59.073
is fatter and less known.

00:02:00.373 --> 00:02:01.833
Here's your checklist.

00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:03.368
Do it now, for real.

00:02:03.368 --> 00:02:07.893
First, every market's collateral, oracle and LTV listed.

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:12.908
Next, Loss probability and loss-given-default written down (your assumptions).

00:02:12.908 --> 00:02:16.768
Then, Utilisation alert (e.g. > 90%).

00:02:16.768 --> 00:02:19.355
And finally, Per-vault and per-curator caps.

00:02:20.656 --> 00:02:21.866
Question 1.

00:02:21.866 --> 00:02:28.151
Borrow APY 10%, utilisation 70%, reserve factor 10%.

00:02:28.151 --> 00:02:29.621
Supply APY?

00:02:34.041 --> 00:02:36.126
The answer: 6.3%.

00:02:37.426 --> 00:02:38.671
Question 2.

00:02:38.671 --> 00:02:42.516
Why is utilisation at 100% a lender's problem?

00:02:46.936 --> 00:02:50.379
The answer: All liquidity is lent out, so withdrawals

00:02:50.379 --> 00:02:53.566
wait until borrowers repay or new deposits arrive.

00:02:54.866 --> 00:02:56.136
Question 3.

00:02:56.136 --> 00:02:58.111
What's a curated vault's extra risk?

00:03:02.531 --> 00:03:05.745
The answer: The curator's allocation decisions and

00:03:05.745 --> 00:03:08.391
permissions, on top of each market's risk.

00:03:09.691 --> 00:03:10.936
Let's recap.

00:03:10.936 --> 00:03:14.066
Collateral quality and liquidation LTV.

00:03:14.066 --> 00:03:16.151
Oracle: what prices the collateral,

00:03:16.151 --> 00:03:18.076
and can it be manipulated?

00:03:18.076 --> 00:03:19.971
(Module 5.3).

00:03:19.971 --> 00:03:22.691
Utilisation: near 100% means you may

00:03:22.691 --> 00:03:24.756
not be able to withdraw.

00:03:24.756 --> 00:03:28.809
Curator: track record, how allocations are changed, timelocks.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:32.624
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:32.624 --> 00:03:36.649
Next up, Lesson 12.6: Books, records and succession.
